  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/4f/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_54.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Iron Man is out testing out his new face plate when Pepper summons Stark back to his office.&nbsp; He's wanted at the Pentagon.&nbsp; When he gets there, they complain that his observer missiles in Viet Nam, which they have been using to take pictures behind enemy lines, have all either crashed or disappeared.&nbsp; Stark heads off to Viet Nam in person to take a look and begins to suspect the Mandarin's hand in this as \"the most brilliant evil genius in the Orient!\"&nbsp; Stark knows that the Mandarin has no love for the Reds either.&nbsp; \"His objective is to destroy <i>all</i> military might, so that <i>his</i> power will be supreme!\"\n\nHe decides to allow himself to be captured as Tony Stark, knowing that an all-out frontal assault by Iron Man would draw the Mandarin's defenses immediately.&nbsp; Thus, he gets into a Jeep and brazenly drives to the Mandarin's \"mountaintop castle,\" where he is soon captured by its guards.&nbsp; Their attempt to open his attache case causes them to activate a sleep gas booby trap, giving Stark time to change to Iron Man before Mandarin shows up in person to interrogate him.\n\nThe two engage, and after Iron Man repeatedly throws off his various attacks, the Mandarin actually offers to ally himself with him.&nbsp; \"Serve me -- and together we can rule the world!&nbsp; For your power is second only to <i>mine</i>!\"\n\nThe armored Avenger refuses, but soon the battle takes its toll on Iron Man's power levels, and he needs a recharge fast!&nbsp; But he has no opportunity, and the Mandarin quickly snares him in magnetic steel cables that cling to his iron suit.&nbsp; He's trapped, knowing that if he had a full charge, he could snap them, but his power is now almost completely depleted.\n\n\"The time has <i>come</i>, Iron Man!&nbsp; If you know any prayers, say them now, while you still can!\"\n\n[CONTINUED]",
  "grade_one_liner": "Thin on plot, rich in unintentional comedy and over-the-top dialogue,\nthis one was far more entertaining than it had any right to be.",
  "subtitle": "In for a Penny...",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | NewFacePlate | width=11%] The cover blurb (and even the upper-left corner image) announces Iron Man's new \"Protective Head Mask\" and now features rivets instead of the spiked -- or horned -- look.\n[BR]\n[BR] And as the inner panel indicates, he is just now returning from his first time out with the new helmet.\n\nWriter Stan Lee uses the premise of the disappearing \"observer missiles\" to move Iron Man over to Viet Nam in order to get him closer to this month's villain:\n[ASSET: center | ObserverMissileDisappearing | width=large]\n\nIn and of itself, this is no problem, as one would <i>expect</i> a weapons manufacturer such as Tony Stark to be involved with the war effort overseas (and indeed, as readers know, he <i>became</i> Iron Man as a result of events that happened there).&nbsp; Once he is on the scene to investigate, he begins to realize that the missiles are being affected by a \"force beam\" whose location Stark is able to detect as originating from the Mandarin's location.\n\nUpon first reading, it seems highly unlikely that Stark would know <i>exactly</i> where to drive in his Jeep to get to the Mandarin's \"mountaintop castle,\" but one glance back at page 5 of [LINK:1963:TOS_50_iron0|TOS #50|2027-03-24] (where Mandarin made his debut) shows that Iron Man did <i>indeed</i> know where it was, thanks to the CIA (though that story indicates it's in Red China).&nbsp; Whether Stark actually drove in a Jeep all the way to China from Vietnam is questionable, but it's probably best from a reviewer's perspective <i>not</i> to split hairs over such a minor plot quirk.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(At least Stan remembered he lived in a <i>castle</i>, but as Stan seems to really like castles <i>anyway</i>, was this just a happy accident?&nbsp; <i>Hmm...</i>)]\n\n[ASSET: left | FinallyFaceToFace | width=14%] It's downright <i>impressive</i> how easily Stan and Heck consume [SPAN: severity=info; text=half the story] with little more than setup before finally getting to this month's showcase battle between Iron Man and the Mandarin.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=Yet oddly enough, this ends up mattering less than I expected, because once the Mandarin finally arrives, the issue begins veering into territory I never saw coming.]\n\n(Oh, and by the way, in case anyone is keeping track, this is the <b><i>sixth</i></b> time in only 7 pages that Stark has either changed <i>into</i> or <i>out of</i> his Iron Man armor.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=He's obviously got it down to a science!])\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | SecondOnlyToMine | width=14%] [ASSET: right | LateShowHoldover | width=14%] The battle begins, but right off the bat, the dialogue between the two seems to lack its usual zest, but in this case, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Stan himself seems to realize this,] because he immediately has Iron Man \"compensate\" by saying (or thinking) about how lame the Mandarin sounds, in one case even marveling how he sounded [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"like a hold-over from the late late show.\"]\n[BR]\n[BR] It's as if Stan Lee realized - [SPAN: severity=warning; text=while he was typing the words] -- just how corny the Mandarin sounded, <i>especially </i>after he offered to join forces with his enemy so that [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=\"we can rule the world!\"]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=I actually found this funny, especially since I was thinking the <b>exact same thing</b> as our hero!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(What a complete <i>doofus!</i>  But far from being nauseated, I found myself in stitches...)]\n\n[ASSET: left | Trapped | width=25%] Self-awareness aside, though, the page count was running its course, and it was time to draw things to a close.&nbsp; As late as page 11, Stan at least had the presence of mind to refer back to why Iron Man paid a call to the Mandarin's stronghold in the first place:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=to find out how he was destroying the observer missiles.]\n[BR]\n[BR] (We'll see if he remembers to mention this again in <i>next</i> month's issue though...)\n\n[DIVIDER]\nFrom that point onward, however, the actual mystery increasingly feels secondary to the spectacle unfolding around it.\n\n[HANG0] As the issue comes to a close, you can almost picture Stan at his typewriter, thinking:\n[BR] \"<i>Heck</i>, in for a penny, in for a <b>pound</b>.\n[BR]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Okay then -- <b>we're doing this!</b>\"]\n\nAnd then he <i>intentionally</i> has the Mandarin spit out about the corniest thing ever, suggesting that his now-helpless enemy actually <b>say his prayers</b>!\n\nThe absolute earnestness of his face only served to elicit even more laughter on my part.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Hahahaha!]\n[ASSET: center | SayYourPrayers | width=large]\n\nIron Man is out of power and only seconds away from certain doom, and where does his mind go?\n[BR] To Happy and Pepper of course, because... <i>well</i>... [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=why not?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(You can't help but laugh some more... you just <i>met</i> these characters only 9 issues ago, and already, they're your go-to faces?)]\n\nFor once, the [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=day is NOT saved], and readers are asked to deposit 12 more cents to continue.\n\n[ASSET: left | BossesAintHuman | width=14%] While I'm on the subject of the supporting characters, let me pause there briefly before I close.\n[BR]\n[BR] As always, they were once again right where they <i>always</i> are -- in Stark's office with Pepper busily doing her secretarial work while Happy just hangs around bothering her, and the two of them apparently do nothing much else.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=While they don't actually do anything wrong,] they increasingly feel shoehorned into stories and provide little payoff beyond sidetracking the actual plot.\n\nAs a result, it's becoming harder to stay invested in them.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis story clearly seemed intended only to serve as a setup for next time -- at least at the start.\n[BR] It was definitely a lightweight tale that contained little actual substance.\n\nLet me make sure I have this right:[BR]\n[HANG] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Iron Man heads to the Orient to meet up once more with the Mandarin,]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=only to be captured just as his power reserves run out.]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Tune in next month for more.]\n\n<i>Yep</i>, I think that about covers it.\n\n<i>Sure</i>, there was the mystery of the disappearing missiles, but they only served as the catalyst for moving Stark overseas, where he would be closer to the Mandarin, whom he suspects of being behind the whole thing, and in that respect, I have no problem with it.\n\nI also don't have any problem with Stark's knowledge of exactly where to find the Mandarin (since he'd already visited his castle once before).&nbsp; He, more than anyone else, would actually have known just where to go, even if it did cause me to do a double-take at first reading (not to mention him being able to drive a Jeep straight into Red China).\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=It was a readable enough tale that I suspect morphed into something entirely different by the end.]\n\nTo me, it contained such <i>outrageous dialogue</i> in places that [SPAN: severity=success; text=I could not help but laugh,] and from there, it almost seemed to get worse.&nbsp; And the worse it got, the more Iron Man commented on it, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=and the more Stan Lee leaned into it with unapologetic abandon!]\n\n[ASSET: left | NauseatingDialogue | width=12%] Stan apparently decided he might as well go out with a bang, and we read:\n[BR]\n[BR] \"If this is to be my finish, I'll show how an <i>American</i> faces death!\n[BR] I'll show that <i>nothing</i> can shatter the faith of a man who fights for freedom!\n[BR] Somehow, even in defeat, I'll be <i>victorious</i>!\"\n\n[HANG0] And somehow, despite its numerous weaknesses, and even though it seems impossible,\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=I had a great time reading it!]\n\n[HANG0] In the end, the abject absurdity of it all finally overwhelmed me.\n[BR] Despite knowing that this <i>shouldn't work</i>...<i> </i>I still surrendered willingly to it... and laughed my head off!\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=I feel like I've stumbled onto the secret of a 60+ year cold case.]",
  "side_note": [
    "[DEVNOTE: shellhead]\n\n[HANG0] The cover (as well as page 2) seem bound and determined to establish Iron Man's nickname as \"Bullet-Head\" as opposed to \"Shell-Head.\"&nbsp; Recall that the letters page of [LINK:1964:FF_25|FF #25|2027-05-10] discussed this as well, with Marvel suggesting that readers have \"more respect for our heroes.\"&nbsp; But I think the cat is already out of the bag.&nbsp; The \"Shell-head\" name was already used by the Hulk in [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08].&nbsp; So my belief is that Marvel is fighting a losing battle.\n\n[HANG0] This issue ends with the announcement that there's more to come in next issue's \"Iron Man #55.\"&nbsp; Of course, the name of the actual magazine is <i>Tales of Suspense</i>, but as indicated in the Special Announcements in this month's [LINK:1964:ASM_13|ASM #13|2027-07-01], Marvel has already stated that it will \"refer to it as IRON MAN from now on.\"&nbsp; (I discuss this more in [SUMMARY: 1964-03 | this month's summary].)\n\n[HANG0] Iron Man flies to Washington DC in \"less than an hour\" (page 4) using a combination of flying and jet-powered \"roller wheels.\"&nbsp; That's a pretty impressive speed, as it's [SPAN: severity=info; text=over 200 miles from Flushing, New York to DC,] so I sure <i>hope</i> that he spent a good portion of his time in the air since I personally would <i>not</i> want to be zooming around on roller wheels at over 200 miles per hour!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Gasp!]\n\n[HANG0] To conserve energy, Iron Man hitches a ride on a long-range ICBM that is being shot over the Pacific Ocean.&nbsp; This is consistent with other stories where the still-relatively-new Iron Man elects not to use up his own power in flight (e.g. [LINK:1963:TOS_42_iron0|TOS #42]).\n\n[HANG0] Tony Stark is pictured smoking a pipe in the first panel on page 5 -- and again in a Jeep on page 6.\n\n[HANG0] The story's final panel is confusing, reading:&nbsp; \"Yes, we <i>could</i> have <b>ended</b> our tale at this point -- but there is just too much action, too much drama, too much excitement for us to rush it!\" (and so please come back in next month)\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Um... excuse me, but... didn't you just do <b>exactly that?</b>]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Perhaps they <i>meant</i> to say that they could have <b>continued</b> the story...)]",
    "<b>Tales of the Watcher:</b>  \"Hands Off!\"\n(by Stan Lee, Larry Lieber and Chic Stone)\n\nThe story picks up after the origin tale from the previous month, where we learn why the Watcher's race must only passively observe, but it causes him great agony to watch, knowing that he could actually intervene to save this race or that planet.\n\nHowever, he eventually comes across a civilization that elects to shoot its atomic waste into space rather than let it infect their own planet, knowing that eventually, \"it will hit another celestial body... it will put an end to all life there!\"&nbsp; For decades, the Watcher checks back in on this floating time bomb until eventually, \"the cosmic space tides are forcing it toward a group of inhabited planets!\"&nbsp; If it lands on one of these, he knows it will destroy all life there.\n\nHe knows he has the power to stop it, but he is forbidden, and we see his anguish over the situation!&nbsp; \"If I do not act, millions will die!!&nbsp; And if I do act, I will be defying my most sacred oath!\"&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: As Ben Grimm might say:&nbsp; Wotta revoltin' development this is!\"]\n\nBut at that moment, a faraway super nova knocks one of this system's uninhabited planets out of its orbit right toward one of the inhabited ones, but wait!&nbsp; It strikes the radioactive atomic waste cloud, destroying both the runaway planet and the cloud.&nbsp; Holy cow, everyone is saved after all!&nbsp; Had the Watcher intervened and removed the cloud, the planet would have crashed into one of the inhabited worlds!&nbsp; \"Thus, by keeping my vow and doing nothing, I have saved an entire population!\"\n\nHis father was right after all!&nbsp; He must never intervene -- only look, listen and record!"
  ],
  "highlight": "Iron Man faces a castle-dwelling supervillain and increasingly outrageous dialogue in a surprisingly entertaining Mandarin rematch."
}